The standard algorithm for finding the next lexicographical permutation
modifies the permutation in-place in linear time (\(O(n)\)) and
constant space (\(O(1)\)).

It can be broken down into four steps: Algorithm overview 

Find the pivot: Starting from the right, find the first element that
is smaller than the element to its right.
This is your "pivot" element, which needs to be replaced.

Handle the last permutation: If no such element is found,
it means the permutation is in descending order (e.g., [5, 4, 3, 2, 1])
and is the lexicographically last one.


In this case, reverse the entire sequence to get the first
(sorted ascending) permutation.Find t

Search the elements to the right of the pivot for the
smallest number that is still greater than the pivot.
Swap and reverse:
    Swap the pivot with this successor element.
    Then, reverse the entire sub-array of elements that is
    to the right of the pivot.

    Example: Finding the next permutation of [1, 3, 5, 4, 2]
    Find the pivot::: A[i] < A[i+1]
    Scan from right to left:2 is not smaller than its right neighbor (it has none).
    4 is not smaller than 2.
    5 is not smaller than 4.
    3 is smaller than 5. We have found our pivot, which is 3 at index 1.

    Find the successor: smallest number greater than 3

    Scan(left to right) the elements to the right of 3 ([5, 4, 2]) for the smallest
    number greater than 3:
    5 is greater than 3.
    4 is greater than 3.
    2 is not greater than 3.
    The smallest number greater than 3 is 4.
    Swap:
    Swap the pivot (3) with the successor (4).
    The array [1, 3, 5, 4, 2] becomes [1, 4, 5, 3, 2].
    Reverse the suffix:
    Reverse the sub-array starting from the position after the pivot
    (index 2), which is [5, 3, 2].
    Reversing this sub-array gives [2, 3, 5].
    The final result is [1, 4, 2, 3, 5].

    C++ standard library::
    The C++ Standard Template Library (STL) provides a
    built-in function
    std::next_permutation in the <algorithm> header that
    implements this exact algorithm.
    It modifies the sequence in-place and returns
    true if a next permutation was found,
    or false if the sequence was already the last permutation.

